# sixfiveohtwo stage 6 expectations.
# Interrupts: masked IRQ (I=1), unmasked IRQ, NMI (unmaskable), BRK
# (pushes PC+2 with B=1), RTI flag restore, ISR register preservation.
#
# P records drive the active-low irq_n (I) / nmi_n (N) pins right
# after the preceding marker is observed. The tb waits on the per-source
# counters ($30 irq, $31 nmi, $32 brk) inside the ISRs to deassert the
# irq level before RTI restores I=0 in phases 2 and 5 -- the pin is
# level-sensitive, so a still-asserted level would re-trigger the IRQ
# the moment RTI restores I=0.

# --- phase 1: IRQ level asserted while I=1 -> masked ---
# Vectors written at runtime: $FFFE/$FFFF = irq_isr ($0276),
# $FFFA/$FFFB = nmi_isr ($028D).
M F001 11
F I 1
C FFFE 76
C FFFF 02
C FFFA 8D
C FFFB 02
P I 0

# Marker 2: main continued through the asserted level; irq counter
# still 0 because I=1 masks it.
M F002 22
F I 1
F N 0
F Z 0
C 30 00
P I 1

# --- phase 2: CLI unmasks -> IRQ taken, RTI restores I=0 ---
M F003 33
F I 0
P I 0

# Wait inside the ISR (irq counter just incremented to 1), then
# deassert the level so the restored I=0 cannot re-trigger.
M 30 01
P I 1

# Marker 4: after RTI. I=0 restored from the pushed P (entry had set
# I=1); SP unwound; X/Y preserved by the ISR; stacked P captured with
# B=0, I=0, N=V=D=Z=C=0 -> $20.
M F004 55
F I 0
R A 55
R X 44
R Y 00
R S FD
C 30 01
C F110 20

# --- phase 3: NMI edge taken despite I=1 ---
M F005 66
F I 1
P N 0

# Marker 6: after the NMI ISR and RTI. The edge was taken although
# I=1; stacked P captured with B=0, I=1 -> $24; irq counter untouched.
M F006 77
F I 1
R A 77
R X 44
R Y 00
R S FD
C 30 01
C 31 01
C F111 24
P N 1

# --- phase 4: BRK pushes PC+2 with B=1; RTI resumes at brk_cont ---
# Marker 7: stored by the continuation at brk_cont ($0245), proving
# the padding byte was skipped. $32=1 proves the padding (itself a
# BRK opcode) was never executed. The ISR captured the stacked P
# ($B4: N=1, I=1, B=1) and the stacked PC = $0245 exactly, and the
# vector was restored to irq_isr.
M F007 88
F I 1
R A 88
R X 44
R Y 00
R S FD
C 32 01
C F112 B4
C F113 45
C F114 02
C FFFE 76
C FFFF 02

# --- phase 5: RTI restores N/C/I; ISR preserves A/X/Y ---
# Flags at the marker: CLI (I=0), SEC (C=1), LDA #$80 (N=1, Z=0).
M F008 80
F N 1
F Z 0
F C 1
F I 0
P I 0

# Wait inside the ISR (irq counter incremented to 2), then deassert
# the level before RTI restores I=0.
M 30 02
P I 1

# Marker 9: after RTI. The PHP/PLA byte $B1 (N=1, C=1, I=0, B=1,
# bit5=1) proves RTI restored C/N and pulled I=0 from the stack
# instead of leaving the interrupt's I=1. A/X/Y registers still hold
# the pre-interrupt values the ISR preserved. Stacked P captured by
# the ISR: $A1 (N=1, C=1, I=0, B=0).
M F009 B1
F N 1
F Z 0
F C 1
F V 0
F D 0
F I 0
R A B1
R X 66
R Y 33
R S FD
C 30 02
C F110 A1
C F120 80
C F121 66
C F122 33
C F123 B1

# --- final marker + complete final snapshot ---
# clc (C=0), lda #$E5 (N=1, Z=0). I is still the RTI-restored 0.
# All interrupt frames unwound: SP=$FD. PC = $0276, the address after
# the final 3-byte sta $F000 at $0273.
M F000 E5
F N 1
F Z 0
F C 0
F V 0
F D 0
F I 0
R A E5
R X 66
R Y 33
R S FD
R P 0276
C 30 02
C 31 01
C 32 01
C 4E 66